[{:db/id 17592186198275 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-46ba7fa0-d231-5401-8252-d4e156d84cf4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198806 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS=[-1,1]\\times [0,1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in [-1,1],\\,y\\in [0,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{0}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $0$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{0}=\\{(0,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,1]\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/1c/1cc30fcc7543bfbbf8c5c59d1436eb3b1a0799d60c9ad1d899957e8fd17d58ab.png)\n\nNow, consider a general ${S}_{i}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some $i\\in [-1,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{i}=\\{(i,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [0,1]\\}\n$$\n\nFor each $i\\in [-1,1],$ ${S}_{i}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,1).$\n\n![](images/49/494c9d77b0ff339b90792270c9d641054a03a2918e3c56a93747b42a77ee3ccf.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $I=[-1,1]:$\n\n$$\nS={⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}.\n$$\n\nAs $i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following rectangle that represents the union ${⋃}_{i\\in I}{S}_{i}:$\n\n![](images/7c/7c5ac5dad0aaacb4347c260d634e6bcd2d6102b25d51813fe106803417cc7539.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287787 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=Z\\times [-1,0]\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a integer number and whose $y$-coordinate belongs to $[-1,0].$\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in Z,\\,y\\in [-1,0]\\}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{-1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is in $[-1,0].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}=\\{(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in [-1,0]\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/63/63199d9f35c331339e97ad3b2094a520d1fb6a9892bd0beac83538c43b9e2748.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is in $[-1,0].$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in R\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/03/03a6a90b3eff572ceed341de55a0d5e625e338ca18f2ae8ee85eed3bb5278167.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $…,{S}_{-3},{S}_{-2},{S}_{0},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & =…{S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1}\\cup {S}_{0}\\cup {S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}… \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in Z}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $Z\\times [-1,0]$ as the set of vertical lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/9a/9a87c5ea8ceaa8a64e30b6375a8bcaa58182ac2e97d68b44c70a0a964c4727dd.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-46ba7fa0-d231-5401-8252-d4e156d84cf4" :question/id #uuid "46ba7fa0-d231-5401-8252-d4e156d84cf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-184533" :question/problem "Let $I=[0,2]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times [-1,1].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}=\\{x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I\\}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}=[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-1,1],b\\in [-1,1]\\}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a rectangle whose vertices are at $(-1,-1),$ $(-1,1),$ $(1,1),$ and $(1,-1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/71/712970edb9966d6544cbe4a360aaa0de3436f356c4c7f23ff601c70ef168a521.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=[-i,i]\\times [-1,1]=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in [-i,i],b\\in [-1,1]\\}\n$$\n\nis the rectangle whose vertices are at $(-i,-1),$ $(-i,1),$ $(i,1),$ and $(i,-1).$\n\n![](images/68/68f8c51062bd9d3ecadd33b0a67ea0990f631375aba9f9cc705946c79abf47d0.png)\n\nThe intersection of all these rectangles is the line segment $\\{0\\}\\times [-1,1]:$\n\n![](images/ff/ffaa9807fa5155dcc848d68bed93a16b267045fae3f5d4d0945e064ad5c11eef.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b5e7343a-4633-545d-a3b9-3c2ada585e3e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b5e7343a-4633-545d-a3b9-3c2ada585e3e" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5e7343a-4633-545d-a3b9-3c2ada585e3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-89fe3c93-58d2-564c-8818-f614741ab1a0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-99bf3628-2a02-53d8-922c-2471074f7424" "answer-2b353f4d-b6c1-544c-a565-cbc81d0130e6" "answer-d3489613-d07f-57e1-bd54-7d15cf117eab" "answer-9a8e4cd5-94f1-51c7-aff4-ee1d59197f9a" "answer-89fe3c93-58d2-564c-8818-f614741ab1a0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99bf3628-2a02-53d8-922c-2471074f7424" :answer/id #uuid "99bf3628-2a02-53d8-922c-2471074f7424" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/0a/0a655218c2ee2a93e2c37601b8d989f449395270fbea8ff2dda34caedccd95e1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b353f4d-b6c1-544c-a565-cbc81d0130e6" :answer/id #uuid "2b353f4d-b6c1-544c-a565-cbc81d0130e6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/49/4